The standard way is a JAX-WS `SOAPHandler<SOAPMessageContext>`, registered on the client or server. It sees the full SOAP envelope, so you can log both directions.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.util.*;
import javax.xml.namespace.QName;
import javax.xml.ws.handler.*;
import javax.xml.ws.handler.soap.*;
import javax.xml.transform.*;
import javax.xml.transform.stream.*;
import javax.xml.transform.dom.DOMSource;

public class LoggingHandler implements SOAPHandler<SOAPMessageContext> {
    private static void dump(SOAPMessageContext ctx, boolean outbound) {
        try {
            System.out.println(outbound ? "--- request ---" : "--- response ---");
            ctx.getMessage().writeTo(System.out);
            System.out.println();
        } catch (Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }

    public boolean handleMessage(SOAPMessageContext ctx) {
        dump(ctx, (Boolean) ctx.get(MessageContext.MESSAGE_OUTBOUND_PROPERTY));
        return true;
    }
    public boolean handleFault(SOAPMessageContext ctx) { dump(ctx, true); return true; }
    public void close(MessageContext ctx) {}
    public Set<QName> getHeaders() { return Collections.emptySet(); }
}
```

Register it:

- **Client:** `((BindingProvider) port).getBinding().setHandlerChain(List.of(new LoggingHandler()));`
- **Server:** put a `@HandlerChain(file = "handlers.xml")` on your service class, or set the chain on the `Endpoint`.

Two notes: `getHeaders()` must return the headers your handler understands, not `null`. And `writeTo` consumes nothing, so it's safe to call on both inbound and outbound. If you only need metadata, read `ctx.getMessage().getSOAPPart()` or the HTTP headers from `MessageContext.HTTP_REQUEST_HEADERS`.

