`window.onload` is the widely supported one. `document.onload` is not a real event handler — it doesn't fire in any browser.

To run code after the page loads, use one of these:

```js
window.onload = function () { /* ... */ };
// or, better:
window.addEventListener('load', function () { /* ... */ });
```

That said, `load` waits for everything including images, so it's often slower than you need. If you just need the DOM ready:

```js
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function () { /* ... */ });
```

`DOMContentLoaded` is supported in all modern browsers and fires earlier. For most scripts, that's the better choice.

For Next.js 13+ with the App Router, use the official `@next/third-parties` package — it's the simplest option.

```bash
npm install @next/third-parties
```

Then in your root layout:

```tsx
import { GoogleAnalytics } from '@next/third-parties/google'

export default function RootLayout({ children }) {
  return (
    <html lang="en">
      <body>{children}</body>
      <GoogleAnalytics gaId="G-XXXXXXXXXX" />
    </html>
  )
}
```

That's it. It handles page-view tracking on route changes automatically.

**If you're on the older Pages Router**, add the script manually in `pages/_app.tsx`:

```tsx
import Script from 'next/script'

export default function App({ Component, pageProps }) {
  return (
    <>
      <Component {...pageProps} />
      <Script
        src="https://www.googletagmanager.com/gtag/js?id=G-XXXXXXXXXX"
        strategy="afterInteractive"
      />
      <Script id="ga" strategy="afterInteractive">
        {`
          window.dataLayer = window.dataLayer || [];
          function gtag(){dataLayer.push(arguments);}
          gtag('js', new Date());
          gtag('config', 'G-XXXXXXXXXX');
        `}
      </Script>
    </>
  )
}
```

With the Pages Router you also need to fire page views on route change yourself, since it's a SPA.

Put your measurement ID in an environment variable (`NEXT_PUBLIC_GA_ID`) rather than hardcoding it. One caveat: if you care about GDPR, you'll need a consent banner before the script loads — GA sets cookies by default.

