When you're using Sequelize, a popular promise-based Node.js ORM, and an error occurs during database operations, Sequelize will throw an error object which contains various properties including the error message and stack trace. However, Sequelize does not provide the filename or the location where the error occurred directly.

To get the filename or the location where the error occurred, you can make use of the stack trace property available in the error object. Here's an example of how you can extract the filename and line number from the stack trace:
```javascript
try {
  // Sequelize operation that might throw an error
  await sequelize.sync();
} catch (error) {
  // Extract the filename and line number from the stack trace
  const stackTrace = error.stack.split('\n');
  const filename = stackTrace[2].split('/').pop(); // This assumes the filename is the last element in the path
  const lineNumber = stackTrace[2].split(':')[1];

  // Log the error message, filename, and line number
  console.error(`Sequelize error: ${error.message} in ${filename} at line ${lineNumber}`);
}
```
In this example, we catch the error thrown by Sequelize and then extract the filename and line number from the stack trace. Note that the index of the stack trace element that contains the filename and line number might vary depending on the environment and the version of Node.js you're using. In this example, we assume that the filename and line number are available in the third element of the stack trace. You might need to adjust the index based on your specific use case.

Additionally, keep in mind that the stack trace might contain sensitive information such as file paths and line numbers that you might not want to expose in a production environment. You should consider sanitizing the stack trace before logging it to ensure that you're not exposing any sensitive information.

