I'm trying to set get id of all elements in an ```HTMLCollectionOf```. I wrote the following code:
```var list = document.getElementsByClassName("events");
console.log(list[0].id);
for (key in list) {
    console.log(key.id);
}
```
But I got the following output in console:
```event1
undefined
```
which is not what I expected. Why is the second console output ```undefined``` but the first console output is ```event1```?

chosen:
`for...in` iterates over the *keys* of the object, not the elements. For an `HTMLCollectionOf`, those keys are strings like `"0"`, `"1"`, `"length"`, `"item"`, `"namedItem"`. So `key` is `"0"`, and `key.id` is `undefined` because strings don't have an `id`.

You want the value, not the key:

```js
var list = document.getElementsByClassName("events");
for (var key in list) {
    console.log(list[key].id);
}
```

Better still, use a plain indexed loop, which is clearer and avoids inherited properties:

```js
var list = document.getElementsByClassName("events");
for (var i = 0; i < list.length; i++) {
    console.log(list[i].id);
}
```

Or, if you want to be modern about it, `Array.from(list).forEach(el => console.log(el.id))`.

