`try/catch` only catches a rejected promise if you `await` it (or return it from an async function). A bare promise rejection is not caught by the surrounding `try`.

This works:

```js
async function load() {
  try {
    const res = await fetch(url);   // rejection is caught here
    return await res.json();
  } catch (err) {
    console.error(err);
  }
}
```

This does not:

```js
try {
  fetch(url);        // no await, so the rejection escapes
} catch (err) {
  // never runs
}
```

The fix is either `await` the promise, or attach `.catch()`:

```js
fetch(url).catch(err => console.error(err));
```

