It seems like the issue you're facing is related to how the browser handles URLs when they are directly entered into the address bar. When you directly enter a URL into the address bar, the browser doesn't have the context of the application's state, which is why you're seeing the unexpected behavior.

One way to handle this is by using a server that supports HTML5 history API. When a user directly enters a URL, the server should return the main HTML file of your application, and then the client-side routing will take over and render the correct component based on the URL.

However, since you mentioned that you're not using a server yet, you can use the HashRouter from 'react-router-dom' instead of the BrowserRouter. The HashRouter uses the hash portion of the URL (i.e., window.location.hash) to keep the UI in sync with the URL. This means that even if a user directly enters a URL, the application will still be able to route correctly.

Here's how you can use HashRouter:

```jsx
import { HashRouter as Router, Routes, Route } from 'react-router-dom';

