Use `java.security.MessageDigest` with the algorithm name `"MD5"`. Stream the whole file through it, then format the digest as hex.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.security.MessageDigest;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance("MD5");
        try (InputStream in = new FileInputStream("example.txt")) {
            byte[] buf = new byte[8192];
            int n;
            while ((n = in.read(buf)) != -1) {
                md.update(buf, 0, n);
            }
        }
        byte[] hash = md.digest();

        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        for (byte b : hash) {
            sb.append(String.format("%02x", b));
        }
        System.out.println(sb.toString());
    }
}
```

Two things worth knowing:

- There is no `java.util.MD5` class — only `MessageDigest`.
- MD5 is cryptographically broken (collisions are easy to produce). It's fine for checksums against accidental corruption, but for security use SHA-256 instead — just change `"MD5"` to `"SHA-256"`.

For large files, the streaming loop above matters: reading the whole file into one array will fail on big inputs.

