To create a React app that re-renders a map function, you'll need to use state and the `useState` hook to manage the data that the map function is rendering. Here's a simple example of a React app that renders a list of items and allows you to add new items to the list:

1. First, make sure you have a recent version of Node.js installed. You can download it from <https://nodejs.org/>.
2. Next, create a new React app using `create-react-app`:
```
npx create-react-app react-map-rerender
cd react-map-rerender
```
3. Replace the contents of `src/App.js` with the following code:
```javascript
import React, { useState } from 'react';

function App() {
  const [items, setItems] = useState([]);

  const addItem = () => {
    setItems([...items, { id: items.length, name: `Item ${items.length + 1}` }]);
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={addItem}>Add Item</button>
      <ul>
        {items.map((item) => (
          <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;
```
This code creates a simple React app with a button that adds a new item to the `items` array when clicked. The `items` array is stored in the component's state using the `useState` hook. The `map` function is used to render a list of items, and each item is given a unique `key` prop to help React optimize rendering.

4. Start the app by running `npm start` in the terminal. You should see a button labeled "Add Item" and an empty list. Click the button to add new items to the list.

Each time you click the "Add Item" button, the `addItem` function is called, which updates the `items` state with a new item. This triggers a re-render of the component, causing the `map` function to re-render the list of items with the new item included.

